{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The desired theorem may be false even for two arms; a two-arm proof may also fail to extend to dyadic market windows.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","counterexample search","algorithm design"],"summary":"Settle the abstract fast/slow delayed advantage game. In the model with fast reward observed immediately, slow reward observed after delay D, slow-play probability q_t, Delta_t=s_t-f_t, A_T=sum q_t Delta_t, and S_T=sum Delta_t, either prove an algorithm with A_T >= -polylog*sqrt(T) and A_T >= S_T - polylog*sqrt(TD), or build an adversarial Delta_t sequence refuting it.","title":"Two-Arm Delayed Advantage","why_promising":"This is the smallest unresolved core of the tail-adaptive truncation program. A proof would likely imply the missing comparator-specific delayed experts ingredient; a counterexample would redirect the upper-bound search."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The oracle may require knowing the law P or may only prove a pathwise charge much larger than delta_T(H).","suggested_lenses":["finite example search","oracle inequality","pathwise versus expected values"],"summary":"Test the nested-window adaptation route on two-window, two- or three-quote finite reward tables. Use horizon-censored nonnegative increments z_t^m, keep expected comparator values V_m^G(P), and check whether any causal algorithm can satisfy an oracle of the form V_m^G - E L^m(A) <= B_m + c(V_M^G - V_m^G).","title":"Nested-Window Oracle Stress Test","why_promising":"The market rewards have monotone nested structure that generic heterogeneous-delay experts ignores. Small finite tables can quickly reveal whether this structure is enough or whether the expected-oracle target is impossible."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Information leakage may force a KL/gap tradeoff that is too weak; independent signs may again collapse against one fixed comparator.","suggested_lenses":["Bayes value computation","information lower bound","finite-grid abstraction"],"summary":"Design a finite-grid noisy common-type or codebook lower-bound family before realizing it as prices. Compute the exact finite-grid Psi_G value under a prior, quantify terminal KL/TV leakage, and see whether many partially informative delayed events can accumulate a Bayes gap at the power-tail upper-bound scale.","title":"Noisy Codebook Lower Bounds","why_promising":"The one-block qD atom is too weak, and naive repetition fails. The finite-grid minimax identity gives a precise diagnostic for whether a more subtle lower-bound family has real fixed-comparator value."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Quote localization may destroy the fixed comparator or make all valuable blocks too small; cross-block hits may still violate the target tail envelope.","suggested_lenses":["market realization","tail-envelope audit","counterexample search"],"summary":"Independently study how to realize multi-block lower-bound abstractions as actual price paths despite persistent old quotes. Try geometrically separated quote levels, blocker prices, or decreasing-scale blocks, and explicitly compute beta, Delta, delta, and cross-block hitting probabilities.","title":"Persistent-Quote Localization","why_promising":"Even a good finite-grid lower-bound abstraction is useless unless it survives the protocol's persistent quotes. This direction isolates the realization obstruction from the information-theoretic design."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The calculus may only repackage known delta/Delta bounds and cannot by itself overcome the hidden/labeled no-go.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","formula derivation","counterexample audit"],"summary":"Develop the last-exceedance identity into exact formulas or useful bounds for truncation loss. Express full value through L_a=max{s<=T:P_s>a} and truncated value through recent exceedance ages, then look for value-tail sufficient conditions weaker than raw beta and compare them on rare-history and tiny-price examples.","title":"Last-Exceedance Value Tails","why_promising":"It targets the right payoff-weighted object: fixed-quote value is governed by last future exceedances, not raw first hitting probabilities. It may yield cleaner upper bounds or sharper examples."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"This may be mostly theorem hygiene rather than new mathematics; standard measurable-version facts still need careful citation or proof.","suggested_lenses":["measure theory","proof audit","theorem polishing"],"summary":"Close the payoff-measure dominated infinite-class theorem for the original finite-grid Borel policy model. Prove the reverse completed-L-infinity-kernel-to-Borel implementation, state the randomization and Borel-version lemmas precisely, and verify one consistent policy class is used for finite subclasses and the full class.","title":"Original-Policy No-Gap Closure","why_promising":"The relaxed completed-kernel no-gap theorem is nearly complete. Closing the original-policy version would turn a conditional infinite-class result into a clean theorem package."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"A doubling or randomized horizon-guessing policy may defeat the proposed obstruction; the lower bound must compare to the best fixed quote at each horizon without mixing horizons incorrectly.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","horizon quantifiers","known-law regime"],"summary":"Formalize or refute the anytime known-law obstruction. Use deterministic super-growing two-deadline blocks where one horizon favors quote near 1/2 and a later horizon favors quote near 1, and prove whether every single horizon-free policy has linear regret along some horizon subsequence.","title":"Anytime Known-Law Boundary","why_promising":"The known-law oracle is horizon-aware. Settling the anytime variant would clarify whether nontrivial tail or deadline conditions reappear even for known exogenous processes."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
